      "id": "sync-and-counterfactual",
      "status": "PASS",
      "judgment": "Both languages explicitly mark instantaneous-signal synchronization as hypothetical, retain the 0:45 example, distinguish local event readings from seeing a distant flash, and account for finite radio-signal delay."
    },
    {
      "id": "spacecraft-clock-experiment",
      "status": "PASS",
      "judgment": "Both languages retain fixed endpoint clocks and midpoint emission. Equal rest-frame distances and opposite-direction light speeds justify simultaneous receptions in K_1. In K the endpoints move relative to the fixed emission position while light continues at c in both directions, giving rear-first reception."
    },
    {
      "id": "synchronization-not-rate",
      "status": "PASS",
      "judgment": "Both texts explicitly deny unequal ticking rates or clock malfunction. The difference is between the synchronization rules used by two inertial frames."
    },
    {
      "id": "relativity-scope",
      "status": "PASS",
      "judgment": "The text restricts the explicit separated-event example to separation along relative motion, rather than asserting that every possible event pair changes simultaneity. Equal physical laws and invariant vacuum light speed are included; finite signal speed alone is explicitly insufficient."
